JOB 22 — One Drawing, Many Machines
Topic: Inheritance
1. Aim
To understand inheritance and code reuse in Python.
2. Inheritance
Inheritance allows a child class to use the properties and methods of a parent class.
Example:
Machine
   |
   ├── Lathe
   └── Press
Lathe and Press can inherit common features from Machine.
3. Example
class Machine:
    def __init__(self, tag):
        self.tag = tag
        self.running = False
        self.hours = 0

    def start(self):
        self.running = True
        return f"{self.tag} started"

    def stop(self):
        self.running = False
        return f"{self.tag} stopped"

    def status(self):
        return f"{self.tag}: running={self.running}"
Child Class
class Lathe(Machine):
    def __init__(self, tag, max_rpm=2000):
        super().__init__(tag)
        self.max_rpm = max_rpm
        self.parts = 0

    def make_part(self):
        if not self.running:
            return "REFUSED: not running"

        self.parts += 1
        return f"{self.tag} made part {self.parts}"
Example
L1 = Lathe("L-01")

print(L1.start())
print(L1.make_part())
print(L1.status())
Output
L-01 started
L-01 made part 1
L-01: running=True
4. super()
super() is used to access the parent class.
super().__init__(tag)
It allows the child class to use the initialization of the parent class.
Key Points
Parent class provides common features.
Child class inherits those features.
super() accesses the parent class.
Inheritance reduces code duplication
